1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,664
One hour in, your friend in
Singapore is already playing GTA 6.

2
00:00:03,664 --> 00:00:07,920
You, in Indonesia, started at the same
second and still have eight hours to

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:08,220
go.

4
00:00:09,253 --> 00:00:13,066
GTA 6 landed on November nineteenth
at up to two hundred and twelve

5
00:00:13,066 --> 00:00:13,683
gigabytes.

6
00:00:13,683 --> 00:00:16,656
Divide that by the middle
speed of Indonesian homes,

7
00:00:16,656 --> 00:00:19,797
assume it never drops, and
you get nine hours in total.

8
00:00:19,797 --> 00:00:24,114
The ocean explains only part of it, and
your own phone is about to embarrass

9
00:00:24,114 --> 00:00:25,067
your home cable.

10
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:27,540
Think of the internet as water.

11
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:30,760
The file is a full tank that
has to reach your house,

12
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:33,380
and your speed is how
hard your pipe flows.

13
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:37,040
A thin pipe fills the
same tank, only later.

14
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:40,796
Follow that pipe from
the sea to your door.

15
00:00:40,796 --> 00:00:44,454
It has three parts: the main
pipe between cities and islands,

16
00:00:44,454 --> 00:00:48,878
the street pipe past your neighbourhood,
and the last hose into one house.

17
00:00:48,878 --> 00:00:53,067
Three players matter too: the state,
which laid part of the main pipe,

18
00:00:53,067 --> 00:00:55,663
the companies that sell
the water, and you,

19
00:00:55,663 --> 00:00:56,240
who pays.

20
00:00:57,333 --> 00:01:01,333
Speed is counted in megabits per second,
which is how much water passes each

21
00:01:01,393 --> 00:01:01,713
second.

22
00:01:02,513 --> 00:01:06,193
The numbers here are medians, the
middle result when every speed test is

23
00:01:06,253 --> 00:01:07,993
lined up from slowest to fastest.

24
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:12,126
So when do you actually get to play?

25
00:01:12,126 --> 00:01:16,772
For home internet, called fixed
broadband, Indonesia's median in September

26
00:01:16,772 --> 00:01:19,435
was about fifty-three megabits per second.

27
00:01:19,435 --> 00:01:22,284
Singapore's was four
hundred and seventy-six,

28
00:01:22,284 --> 00:01:24,081
roughly nine times the flow.

29
00:01:24,081 --> 00:01:27,488
Indonesia ranked one hundred
and twelfth in the world,

30
00:01:27,488 --> 00:01:30,215
and ninth of ten on a
Southeast Asian list,

31
00:01:30,215 --> 00:01:31,633
ahead of only Myanmar.

32
00:01:32,767 --> 00:01:34,627
The neighbours wait far less.

33
00:01:34,627 --> 00:01:39,091
The same file takes about an hour
and a half at Thailand's or Vietnam's

34
00:01:39,091 --> 00:01:42,439
median, and about two and
a half hours at Malaysia's.

35
00:01:42,439 --> 00:01:44,671
Singapore takes fifty-nine minutes.

36
00:01:44,671 --> 00:01:47,957
Indonesia takes eight hours
and fifty-seven minutes.

37
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:52,468
Then your phone offers
a shortcut into GTA 6.

38
00:01:52,468 --> 00:01:56,670
In September's speed rankings,
Indonesia's mobile networks reached about

39
00:01:56,670 --> 00:02:00,354
seventy-one megabits per second,
faster than its home internet.

40
00:02:00,354 --> 00:02:04,498
At that median, your phone would
finish more than two hours before your

41
00:02:04,498 --> 00:02:04,900
cable.

42
00:02:06,053 --> 00:02:07,886
The obvious suspect is the sea.

43
00:02:07,886 --> 00:02:11,954
Singapore covers about seven hundred
and forty-four square kilometres.

44
00:02:11,954 --> 00:02:14,648
Indonesia covers about
one point nine million,

45
00:02:14,648 --> 00:02:17,513
spread over more than
seventeen thousand islands.

46
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:21,100
For every one patch of ground
Singapore has to serve,

47
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:23,600
Indonesia has about two
and a half thousand,

48
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:28,040
and between many of them is water
with a seabed that needs a cable.

49
00:02:29,147 --> 00:02:30,988
Indonesia did lay that pipe.

50
00:02:30,988 --> 00:02:35,241
A state project called the Palapa
Ring carried the fibre backbone,

51
00:02:35,241 --> 00:02:38,477
the main pipe, out to
remote parts of the country.

52
00:02:38,477 --> 00:02:43,363
The eastern section alone has more than
four thousand kilometres of undersea

53
00:02:43,363 --> 00:02:46,407
cable, and the project
was inaugurated in 2019.

54
00:02:47,553 --> 00:02:49,833
That main pipe matters,
and on remote islands,

55
00:02:49,893 --> 00:02:51,053
it can still be the weak point.

56
00:02:51,773 --> 00:02:54,153
But a main pipe only brings
water as far as town.

57
00:02:54,933 --> 00:02:56,373
It has not entered a single house.

58
00:02:58,233 --> 00:03:02,026
So go somewhere with no sea
in the way: West Jakarta,

59
00:03:02,026 --> 00:03:02,658
on Java.

60
00:03:02,658 --> 00:03:07,995
In the first half of 2026 its median
was about seventy megabits per second,

61
00:03:07,995 --> 00:03:11,577
the highest of any Indonesian
city in that report.

62
00:03:11,577 --> 00:03:15,293
That is still almost seven
hours before GTA 6 opens.

63
00:03:17,167 --> 00:03:21,282
Bandung, a major city on the same
island, had about forty-nine.

64
00:03:21,282 --> 00:03:23,467
The sea explains part of the gap.

65
00:03:23,467 --> 00:03:28,288
It cannot explain why cities on
Indonesia's most crowded island still wait

66
00:03:28,288 --> 00:03:29,445
six to ten hours.

67
00:03:30,533 --> 00:03:34,960
The trouble moves to the last hose,
the stretch from the street into your

68
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:35,319
home.

69
00:03:35,319 --> 00:03:38,790
Here every metre has to be
paid for, dug in or strung up,

70
00:03:38,790 --> 00:03:40,166
and then looked after.

71
00:03:40,166 --> 00:03:44,656
A World Bank team estimated in 2022
that seventy to eighty percent of home

72
00:03:44,656 --> 00:03:48,666
broadband costs usually sit in
poles, ducts and cables like these.

73
00:03:49,893 --> 00:03:53,863
One price comes from Indonesia's
internet provider association,

74
00:03:53,863 --> 00:03:56,468
talking about Tangerang, next to Jakarta.

75
00:03:56,468 --> 00:04:01,181
In May, its regional chair put burying
cable at about seventy-five thousand

76
00:04:01,181 --> 00:04:02,297
rupiah per metre.

77
00:04:02,297 --> 00:04:05,335
If three or four cables
share one duct, he said,

78
00:04:05,335 --> 00:04:08,064
it could drop to about
twenty-two thousand.

79
00:04:09,947 --> 00:04:12,733
Singapore built its last
hose as one network,

80
00:04:12,733 --> 00:04:15,276
with a clear answer to who owns the pipe.

81
00:04:15,276 --> 00:04:18,120
Its fibre network was
split into three layers.

82
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:22,236
One company holds the cables and
ducts, another runs the equipment,

83
00:04:22,236 --> 00:04:25,264
and many retailers sell
plans over the same pipe.

84
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:30,739
Plans went on sale in 2010 with
speeds up to one gigabit per second.

85
00:04:30,739 --> 00:04:34,953
By mid 2013, it reached more than
ninety-five percent of homes and

86
00:04:34,953 --> 00:04:35,708
businesses.

87
00:04:35,708 --> 00:04:40,489
In 2024, the government set aside up
to a hundred million Singapore dollars

88
00:04:40,489 --> 00:04:42,500
to push it toward ten gigabits.

89
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:44,880
Indonesia grew differently.

90
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:49,000
The World Bank wrote that sharing
infrastructure was common among mobile

91
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:51,320
networks, but not in home broadband.

92
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:54,920
So each provider tends to
carry its own poles and cables.

93
00:04:56,013 --> 00:04:58,093
Whoever lays your cable has one problem.

94
00:04:58,673 --> 00:05:01,613
Each hose has to earn its money back
from the house at the end of it.

95
00:05:02,513 --> 00:05:04,893
A dense street that can
pay gets the cable first.

96
00:05:05,713 --> 00:05:07,093
A thin or tight street waits.

97
00:05:08,193 --> 00:05:10,673
A phone tower works on
different arithmetic.

98
00:05:10,673 --> 00:05:14,421
One tower covers a whole neighbourhood
with no trench to each door,

99
00:05:14,421 --> 00:05:17,892
and its customers already carry
the receiver in their pockets.

100
00:05:17,892 --> 00:05:21,693
For an operator, that is a quicker
way to sell water to many people.

101
00:05:23,533 --> 00:05:24,833
Now look at it from the living room.

102
00:05:25,853 --> 00:05:29,993
By the Communications Ministry's
figure, only about one household in five

103
00:05:30,053 --> 00:05:31,693
subscribes to fixed home internet.

104
00:05:32,553 --> 00:05:34,893
Many of the others are already
online through a phone.

105
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,388
The providers' association
asked why in its 2026 survey.

106
00:05:39,388 --> 00:05:43,606
About forty-seven percent of those
without it said mobile data already

107
00:05:43,606 --> 00:05:44,795
covers their needs.

108
00:05:44,795 --> 00:05:48,480
About thirty-three percent said
home internet costs too much.

109
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:51,512
The price complaint has a basis.

110
00:05:51,512 --> 00:05:55,858
A figure reported in November puts
fixed internet at about four point nine

111
00:05:55,858 --> 00:05:58,116
percent of national income per person.

112
00:05:58,116 --> 00:06:01,300
The international affordability
target is two percent.

113
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:06,629
Even where the cable is on offer,
people do not automatically take it.

114
00:06:06,629 --> 00:06:10,997
A 2022 World Bank study found that
only twenty-six percent of homes with

115
00:06:10,997 --> 00:06:12,973
access to a provider subscribed.

116
00:06:12,973 --> 00:06:17,400
High cost was the main reason named,
by forty-four percent of households.

117
00:06:19,267 --> 00:06:22,590
Put those pieces together
and a likely loop appears.

118
00:06:22,590 --> 00:06:27,106
Few homes sign up, so the cost of
each cable is split among few people.

119
00:06:27,106 --> 00:06:30,808
The price per home stays hard
to cut, many stay on phones,

120
00:06:30,808 --> 00:06:33,317
and subscribers often pick small plans.

121
00:06:33,317 --> 00:06:36,076
A median measures what
people actually get,

122
00:06:36,076 --> 00:06:40,527
including what they choose to buy,
and fibre itself can go far faster.

123
00:06:41,613 --> 00:06:46,224
On a fifty megabit home plan, GTA 6
cannot arrive in less than roughly nine

124
00:06:46,224 --> 00:06:48,955
and a half hours, however
good the cable is.

125
00:06:48,955 --> 00:06:53,506
On a three hundred megabit plan, the
same sum falls to about an hour and a

126
00:06:53,506 --> 00:06:53,870
half.

127
00:06:55,767 --> 00:06:58,404
To be fair, Singapore
is a lopsided opponent.

128
00:06:58,404 --> 00:07:02,700
It is one city island against a country
stretched across three time zones.

129
00:07:02,700 --> 00:07:06,995
Indonesia's mobile operators have also
asked that mobile speed comparisons

130
00:07:06,995 --> 00:07:10,545
account for when five G arrived
and which frequencies it got.

131
00:07:11,613 --> 00:07:16,053
And a small cheap plan or phone
data alone can be a sensible choice.

132
00:07:16,973 --> 00:07:19,733
For a family that mostly chats
and watches short videos,

133
00:07:20,193 --> 00:07:22,013
paying for a giant pipe adds little.

134
00:07:22,933 --> 00:07:25,633
GTA 6 launch week is not an ordinary week.

135
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:30,464
The last hose is also being
tried without a trench.

136
00:07:30,464 --> 00:07:34,340
Fixed wireless uses a tower to send
internet to a box in your home.

137
00:07:34,340 --> 00:07:38,672
One operator offers it for about a
hundred thousand rupiah a month at up to

138
00:07:38,672 --> 00:07:42,947
a hundred megabits, and reported around
nine hundred thousand customers by

139
00:07:42,947 --> 00:07:43,574
September.

140
00:07:44,767 --> 00:07:48,187
The communications ministry
has also floated a goal:

141
00:07:48,187 --> 00:07:52,510
a hundred megabit home plan for
under two hundred thousand rupiah.

142
00:07:52,510 --> 00:07:56,382
For now that is a goal, and no
national package has a date.

143
00:07:56,382 --> 00:08:00,127
Even at that speed, GTA 6
would need close to five hours.

144
00:08:01,967 --> 00:08:05,926
So your nine-hour wait for GTA 6 has
no single broken cable behind it.

145
00:08:06,827 --> 00:08:10,567
The sea is real, but in Java's
cities, the explanation sits closer.

146
00:08:11,347 --> 00:08:14,027
The price of the last
hose, who shares the pipe,

147
00:08:14,567 --> 00:08:16,367
and what makes sense
for a household to buy.

148
00:08:18,213 --> 00:08:19,873
Think about your friend
in Singapore again.

149
00:08:20,493 --> 00:08:21,653
Your friend did nothing clever.

150
00:08:22,513 --> 00:08:26,252
The pipe to that door was laid as one
shared network years before anyone

151
00:08:26,313 --> 00:08:27,853
needed to pull something
this big through it.

152
00:08:29,733 --> 00:08:32,873
Back in Jakarta, your quicker
route may be in your pocket,

153
00:08:33,313 --> 00:08:34,953
though your data quota would like a word.

154
00:08:35,893 --> 00:08:38,873
The pipe to your door still
matters, and for many doors,

155
00:08:38,933 --> 00:08:41,013
it has yet to arrive at a price that fits.

156
00:08:41,933 --> 00:08:46,593
Next long video: Why more than half of
Indonesians live on a single island.
